      "final_text": "我们的观点：随着油价和收益率上升，市场对经济所受冲击的担忧情绪正在升温。例如，航空燃油价格大幅上涨已直接推高了机票价格，导致以美国运输安全管理局(TSA)安检通道数据衡量的出行量同比下降4.5%。今年早些时候，美国消费者支出曾受到退税刺激的提振，但该效应现已消退，全球能源冲击开始在柴油和航空燃油价格上显现。需求萎缩的初步迹象已在数据中有所体现。我们认为伊朗冲突仍是我们对美国宏观经济相对温和展望的主要尾部风险。至于较高利率是否正在影响经济，我们认为利率已有一段时间处于限制性水平。对利率敏感的行业（如住房、耐用品及建筑投资）自2022年底至2023年以来一直处于收缩区间。劳动力市场亦呈现类似态势，求职成功率持续偏低。那么，为何经济扩张在限制性利率环境下仍得以延续？这主要是因为当前推动增长的核心动力在很大程度上对利率不敏感，包括人工智能(AI)、国防开支增加以及库存重建。政府在这两方面均发挥了重要主导作用，这也是其对利率敏感度较低的部分原因。确实存在某一利率水平可能阻碍人工智能(AI)资本支出建设，但鉴于大型科技公司从更高人工智能(AI)资本支出中所感知到的投资回报，该水平很可能远高于当前利率水平。",
      "iterations": 0,
      "seg_status": "edited",
      "source_text": "What we think With the rise in oil prices and yields, concerns over the impact on the economy are picking up. For example, much higher jet fuel prices have directly impacted the cost of air travel, leading to a -4.5% y/y fall in travel volume as measured by TSA (Transportation Security Administration) checkpoint data. Earlier in the year, US consumer spending was boosted by tax refund stimulus, but now that has run its course, and the global energy shock is starting to show up in diesel and jet fuel. Emerging signs of demand destruction is showing in the data, and we think the Iran conflict remains a key tail risk to our relatively moderate US macroeconomic outlook. As to whether higher rates are impacting the economy, we think they have been restrictive for some time already. Interest rate-sensitive sectors (such as housing, durables, and structures investment) have been in contractionary territory since late 2022 and 2023. The labor market tells a similar story with a low job finding rate. So why has the economic expansion continued despite restrictive rates? This is mainly because the key drivers of growth that have emerged are largely rate-insensitive: AI and greater defense spending and inventory rebuilding. The government has had a heavy hand in both, which is part of why they're less sensitive to rates. There is some level of rates that would derail the AI capex buildout — but it is likely far above where rates sit today, given the return on investment that large tech firms are perceiving from greater AI capex.",
